Activity reports for hang diagnostics (issue #11): what Aurora's own threads are doing.

A Steam Deck log (Linux, Vulkan, Gamescope) showed the game running on at the file select while
nothing was presented any more, and said nothing about where the display loop stopped. Each Aurora
thread kind (the application's host thread inside aurora_begin_frame/aurora_end_frame, the render
worker, the GX FIFO thread, the present-timing waiter) now keeps "what it is doing since when" in a
slot (lib/activity.hpp: two relaxed atomic stores per scope, nested scopes restore the outer one):
waiting for a frame slot or a staging buffer, draining the FIFO, encoding a frame, acquiring the
swap chain texture, submitting, presenting, processing Dawn events, after-submit callbacks, building
the post-process chain, processing GX commands, waiting for a pipeline. aurora_describe_activity()
formats every slot busy for at least N ms into one line for the application's hang watchdog.

The present-timing waiter also counts frames whose GPU completion it gave up on after 5 s
(aurora_present_timing_timeouts, logged at the first and every 100th): without completions the
application's frame-ahead wait (aurora_wait_frame_done) can only end by its timeout.

Test aid AURORA_TEST_ACQUIRE_STALL=<frame>[,<seconds>]: that frame's swap chain acquire takes <seconds>
(default 40), like a swap chain that hands out no image.

